  <fragment slot="dream log">
    When I emerge from whatever state of unawareness I was in, I see that I am
    at some kind of sinister discotheque. I feel relief when I recognize some
    acquaintances in the crowd of partygoers. But when I rest my gaze on any one
    person's features, they contort into something strange and unfriendly.

    <br /><br />It's hard to get oriented in my nauseous state. The room is big
    and then it’s small until it’s big again. No one knows me here. They are
    dancing automatons whose source of movement is unclear. I am the only one
    standing still.

    <br /><br />The taste of metal blooms unpleasantly in my mouth. Eventually
    my unease briefly gives way to a rapturous glimmer. There is something
    beautiful in the danger of this gathering, glowing and radioactive.

    <br /><br />I soon find out why the automatons dance. Whatever compels them
    takes me in as well, but only somatically. Whatever remains of my spirit is
    small and impotent, but aware, within my body. The source of my own movement
    is unclear and relentless.

    <br /><br />When the sun rises, the center of the dance floor collapses and
    everything in sight succumbs to the whirlpool. Lasers bend impossibly
    towards the newly formed void. The ethereal haze that filled the room
    earlier is now a dense river rushing past us. My chest cannot expand against
    the bassline, and the light flares in my vision multiply and expand until I
    cannot see at all.

    <br /><br />A LAPSE

    <br /><br />I am in my apartment and it's Sunday afternoon. I am left with
    an ambient sense of doom and a metallic echo in my mouth.
  </fragment>

  <fragment slot="bio">
    Sofiya V is a person living in New York City.
  </fragment>
</StudyLayout>
